##6. Microservice & Microfrontend
Microservice: A microservice is an architectural style where a large application is broken into many small, independent services, each responsible for one specific thing. 
Ex: in an e-commerce app you might have a separate service for user accounts, another for the product catalog, another for the shopping cart, and another for payments. Each service has its own codebase and database, and they talk to each other over the network (usually via HTTP APIs or message queues). This makes it easier to deploy, scale, and maintain each part independently.

Microfrontend: A microfrontend applies the same idea to the user interface. Instead of one big frontend application, different teams own different parts of the UI
Ex: the header, the search page, and the checkout page — and each can be built and deployed separately. This is especially useful for large organizations where multiple teams work on the same product.

##7. Database: Relational vs non-relational
Relational Database (SQL Database)
A relational database stores data in structured tables with rows and columns — like a very organized spreadsheet. Tables are linked together using relationships (hence the name). You query the data using SQL (Structured Query Language). These databases are great for data with clear structure and relationships, like banking transactions or HR records. Ex: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle.
Non-Relational Database (NoSQL Database)
NoSQL databases store data in more flexible formats — as documents (like JSON), key-value pairs, graphs, or columns. 
They don't require a fixed table structure, so they can handle unstructured or rapidly changing data more easily. They're also often easier to scale horizontally. Examples: MongoDB (document), Redis (key-value), Cassandra (column-wide), Neo4j (graph). Use NoSQL when data is varied, high-volume, or doesn't fit neatly into tables — like user activity logs or social media posts.

##8. API Gateway
An API Gateway is a single-entry point that sits in front of all your backend services. Instead of clients calling each microservice directly, they call the API Gateway, which then routes the request to the right service. 
It acts like a traffic cop and also handles cross-cutting concerns such as authentication (checking if you're logged in), rate limiting (preventing one user from flooding the system), SSL termination, request logging, and response caching. This simplifies the client's job — it only needs to know one address — and keeps security logic in one place. Examples: AWS API Gateway, Kong, NGINX.

##9. Message Queue
A message queue is a way for different parts of a system to communicate asynchronously — meaning the sender doesn't have to wait for the receiver to process the message right away. The sender puts a message into the queue, and the receiver picks it up when ready. This is useful for tasks that take a long time (like sending emails or processing video uploads) or when you want to handle traffic spikes gracefully — the queue absorbs the burst and workers process it at their own pace. Examples: RabbitMQ, Apache Kafka, Amazon SQS. Think of it like a restaurant ticket system: the cashier submits the order (message) and the kitchen (consumer) processes it when they're ready.

