##10. Log & Monitor
Log: A log is a time-stamped record of events that happened in your application — like who logged in, what API was called, or what error occurred. Logs are essential for debugging problems after the fact. You can search them to understand what went wrong. Tools: ELK Stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana), Splunk, CloudWatch Logs.
Monitor: Monitoring is about watching your system in real time and getting alerted when something looks wrong Ex: CPU usage spiking above 90%, response times getting slow, or error rates climbing. Unlike logs (which tell you what happened), monitoring tells you the current health of your system. Tools: Prometheus, Grafana, Datadog, New Relic.
Together, logging and monitoring are how teams know their system is working — and quickly diagnose it when it's not.

##11. Deployment with AWS / Azure / GCP
AWS (Amazon Web Services), Azure (Microsoft), and GCP (Google Cloud Platform) are the three major cloud providers. They offer compute, storage, networking, databases, and hundreds of other managed services. Deploying an application to the cloud typically involves:
•	Packaging your code (into a Docker container or a deployable artifact)
•	Choosing a compute service (like AWS EC2 for virtual machines, AWS ECS/EKS for containers, or AWS Lambda for serverless functions)
•	Setting up networking (VPC, subnets, security groups)
•	Connecting to managed databases (like RDS for SQL or DynamoDB for NoSQL)
•	Configuring CI/CD pipelines so code automatically deploys when changes are pushed
The major benefit of cloud deployment is that you don't need to buy and manage physical hardware — you pay for what you use and can scale instantly.

##12. Security: Authentication and Authorization
Authentication: the process of verifying a user's identity via username/password, biometrics, or an OAuth token from a provider like Google. 

Authorization: checks what permissions you have. Ex: a regular user might be able to read documents, but only an admin can delete them. Ex: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) and claims-based tokens like JWT.

A simple analogy: at a concert, authentication is showing your ID at the door, and authorization is what your ticket type lets you access — general admission vs. VIP backstage.

##13. Why Testing
Testing is how we verify that our code actually does what we intended — and that changing one thing doesn't break something else. There are several types:
•	Unit Tests: Test individual functions or classes in isolation
•	Integration Tests: Test how different components work together
•	End-to-End Tests: Simulate a real user flow through the entire application
•	Regression Tests: Ensure previously fixed bugs don't reappear
Without testing, every change is a leap of faith. Testing saves time in the long run by catching bugs early (when they're cheap to fix), gives developers confidence to refactor code, and serves as documentation of intended behavior. The goal is not 100% coverage for its own sake, but meaningful tests that protect the critical paths of the application.

